On to row 4.

Clear LPT Output.

Before using the LPT in any O/S, you likely want to clear out the previous 
output [if any].  Use this to clear it in anticipation of the next usage, 
etc.

Strip LPT Output.

All operating systems create junk in the LPT: stream for a variety of 
reasons.  This gets rid of all such problems, and prepares the file for 
potential post-processing [such as creation of a .PDF file].

This includes leading and trailing <FF> characters as most post processing 
requires it that way; it could always be edited back in and save if need 
be.  Note: this is a VERY thorough program and it takes some time depending 
on file size.  Elapsed time will be indicated afterwards; this can be 
useful for future cleanups during active development, etc.

Open LPT Output.

the current LPTOUT.TXT file is displayed in your choice of editing 
program.. It can be modified and saved if desired. [Note: Some people just 
want a subset of the file or other editing operation is desired, etc.]

By opening the LPT file before and after the STRIP operation, it can be 
seen first hand what was accomplished.  [In some cases, there is truly 
unbelievable junk in the file, especially if it was processed by the OS/8 
PTP: output handler.  After stripping it is often "day and night" 
differences, etc.

Convert LPT to PDF.

This creates LPTOUT.PDF from the LPTOUT.TXT file [strongly recommended you 
first use the STRIP function!].  Afterwards, the PDF file is opened in the 
current PDF reader program.  [Note: This assumes Adobe Reader 11.x; small 
changes are needed to use an alternative program installed on the Windows 
System.  We do not recommend the newer DC version, but it can be 
accommodated with a small change.  I will document this in the manual, and 
there are other qualifying programs as well [not from Adobe].

Convert LPT to PDF [Greenbar]  Similar to the above, except the print 
includes a background with a very credible simulation of greenbar 
lineprinter paper.  The entire output page is scaled down to fit on 11x8.5 
paper.

Clear PTP Output.
Strip PTP Output.
Open PTP Output.

These are identical to their counterparts targeting the LPT: output stream 
except the targeted file is PTPOUT.TXT, the stream file for the simulated 
paper-tape punch data.  Note: The extensions on the paper-tape-oriented 
files does not necessarily imply text format; however, this makes it easier 
to view when this does apply.  Either or both could be binary data under 
the correct circumstances.

Row 5.

Working Files.

The working files [LPTOUT.TXT, PTPOUT.TXT, PTRIN.TXT etc.] directory is 
opened in its own window.  This allows direct access as required.  [Note: 
PDF descendant files created from LPTOUT.TXT will also be created here.

Configuration Files

The SIMH configuration files are stored in this directory.  By opening this 
directory in its own window, the .ini files can be accessed [and perhaps 
modified].

Active Device Images.

All device image files considered active [and thus mentioned in .ini files 
will be displayed in this directory window.  Depending on instant 
requirements, some of these files may be added from other sources [such as 
e-mail, etc.]

All PEPS Directories.  The directory one level up, PEPS\Folders is opened 
in its own window.  This is needed to allow quicker access to the other 
component directories of PEPS that tend to be only seldom needed [and as 
such do not warrant their own shortcut/icon.

Inactive Device Images.  This is the "mirror image" directory for all of 
the [potentially] useful device image files not currently needed.  By 
having both an active and an inactive directory, the potential for clutter 
is greatly reduced.  Very active users may be moving files from one window 
to another, etc.

Help Documents.  PEPS will include its own documentation, generally in PDF 
format; all files will be accessible by opening this folder.  This will 
allow quick access when necessary, etc.

Swap PTP and LPT files.  Occasionally this is necessary for a variety of 
reasons.  For example, a listing file can be stored in each allowing two 
listings to be compared side by side, etc.

Swap PTR and PTP files.  Occasionally this swap is necessary.  For example, 
P?S/8 PAL may be used to assemble a file that is actually needed in OS/8 
[but cannot be assembled there because P/S/8 PAL is a superior assembler to 
PAL8 in many ways.  [Note: P?S/8 PAL even includes the DEVICE and FILENAME 
directives for OS/8 compatibility despite not much use for them in P?S/8 
Future versions of P?S/8 XPAL [eXtended PAL for the P/S/8 SHELL, perhaps to 
be named PALX] will include features to allow SHELL filename compatibility 
which requires support for 12 character and 4 extension file names.  Means 
will still be provided for OS/8 retrocompatibility, etc.]

P?S/8 BIN can punch the binary output of such an assembly to the PTPOUT.TXT 
file [in DEC paper-tape BIN or RIM format].  Booting to OS/8 after swapping 
these files, The binary is available in OS/8 to be read in from the PTR: 
device and then passed to ABSLDR for loading, etc.

This is the end of the Commands window.  Next some of the other subordinate 
windows that might be called up.

cjl










